#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f4b65e-b93a-51e8-b943-b3b53d47fffb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "7c937587-dc81-5ee9-aa66-a509a87433e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4067" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3$." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n\\log_{2} (2^{x + 1}) &= \\log_{2} (3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_b \\left(b^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\log_{2} (3) \\\\\nx &= \\log_{2} (3) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\log_2 \\left(3 \\right) - 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7784d8-f6a4-5e45-b03e-d76a27372c48" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "45d74b50-fd9d-5d18-835d-a7e140278a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-4069" :question/problem "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5$, find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3^{2x+3} &= 5 \\\\[3pt] \\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right) &= \\log_3 (5) \\\\[3pt] 2x+3 &= \\log_3 (5). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (\\log (5))/(\\log (3)) \\\\\n2x &= (\\log (5))/(\\log (3)) - 3 \\\\\nx &= (\\log (5))/(2\\log (3)) - \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d076e5d-9d08-5355-b813-d1749feb0803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "5d31a3e8-aae6-50b5-9ce1-65cef1d8395e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4071" :question/problem "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9$, find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 6^{x} &= 9 \\\\\n6^{x} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6^x &= 3 \\\\[3pt] \\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right) &= \\log_6 (3) \\\\[3pt] x &= \\log_6 (3) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d076e5d-9d08-5355-b813-d1749feb0803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f69989d-58f3-5c44-9cd6-bba8083f79ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-66832" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to the equation $3({4}^{y})-14=19.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({4}^{y})-14 & =19 \\\\ 3({4}^{y}) & =33 \\\\ {4}^{y} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{4}$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({4}^{y}) & ={log}_{4}(11) \\\\ y & ={log}_{4}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y={log}_{4}(11).$"}]}
